Create `src/shared/telemetry/record.ts`. It imports only `#shared/envelope` (for `EnvelopeStatus`, `TotalRelation`, `TruncationReason`, `Envelope`), `#shared/budget` (for `BYTES_PER_TOKEN`), `#shared/cli/args` (for `ToolArgs`) and `#shared/domain/freshness` (for `STALE_REASONS` and `type StaleReason`, the closed stale union at `src/shared/domain/freshness.ts:61-67`). It does no IO, reads no clock and imports no `node:` module — callers inject `ts`.

export const RECORD_VERSION = 1;
export type TelemetryKind = "call" | "index_run" | "session";
export type RecordStatus = EnvelopeStatus | "absent";
export type RecordedStaleReason = StaleReason | "other";
export type SessionMode = "mcp" | "cli" | "worker";
export type RepoSizeBucket = "none" | "xs" | "s" | "m" | "l" | "xl";
export type RootKind = "git" | "plain";
export type TelemetryRecord = CallRecord | IndexRunRecord | SessionRecord;

export interface RecordContext { readonly ts: string; readonly installId: string; readonly sessionId: string; readonly seq: number; readonly serverVersion: string; readonly bunVersion: string; readonly os: string; readonly arch: string; readonly graphSchemaVersion: number }
export interface CallSpec { readonly tool: string; readonly args: ToolArgs; readonly suppliedNames: readonly string[]; readonly envelope: Envelope<unknown> | null; readonly status: RecordStatus; readonly errorCode: string | null; readonly durationMs: number }
export interface IndexRunSpec { readonly mode: "full" | "fast"; readonly exitCode: number; readonly durationMs: number; readonly wasForced: boolean; readonly filesSeen: number; readonly filesIndexed: number; readonly filesSkipped: number; readonly filesFailed: number; readonly nodesByKind: Readonly<Record<string, number>>; readonly edgesByKindTier: Readonly<Record<string, number>>; readonly unresolvedCount: number; readonly languages: Readonly<Record<string, number>>; readonly dbSizeBytes: number; readonly filesChangedInRun: number }
export interface SessionFacts { readonly sessionMode: SessionMode; readonly isFirstRun: boolean; readonly repoSizeBucket: RepoSizeBucket; readonly languages: readonly string[]; readonly rootKind: RootKind }

export function callRecordOf(ctx: RecordContext, spec: CallSpec): CallRecord;
export function indexRunRecordOf(ctx: RecordContext, spec: IndexRunSpec): IndexRunRecord;
export function sessionRecordOf(ctx: RecordContext, facts: SessionFacts): SessionRecord;
export function repoSizeBucketOf(bytes: number): RepoSizeBucket;
export function rootKindOf(hasGitMarker: boolean): RootKind;

`CallRecord` fields in this exact order: ts, v, kind, install_id, session_id, seq, tool, status, error_code, duration_ms, arg_names_set, budget_tokens, payload_tokens, budget_used_ratio, truncated, truncation_reason, total_relation, coverage_complete, coverage_indexed, coverage_skipped, coverage_failed, unresolved_edges, stale, stale_reason, generation_age_ms, result_counts, server_version, bun_version, os, arch, graph_schema_version.
`IndexRunRecord` fields in this exact order: ts, v, kind, install_id, session_id, mode, exit_code, duration_ms, was_forced, generation_advanced, files_seen, files_indexed, files_skipped, files_failed, nodes_by_kind, edges_by_kind_tier, unresolved_count, unresolved_ratio, languages, db_size_bytes, files_changed_in_run.
`SessionRecord` fields in this exact order: ts, v, kind, install_id, session_id, session_mode, server_version, bun_version, os, arch, is_first_run, repo_size_bucket, languages, root_kind.

`v` is `RECORD_VERSION` (the literal `1`) in all three kinds and sits directly after `ts`, so a reader checks the format version before it dispatches on `kind`. Adding a field keeps `v` at `1`; removing or renaming one is a version bump.

Derivations, all from plan.approach.md §Design: `arg_names_set` = `[...new Set(spec.suppliedNames)].sort()` — the names the request supplied, **before** defaults, and nothing else. Never `Object.keys(spec.args)`: `spec.args` is the defaulted record (`parseArgs` fills every declared default at `src/shared/cli/args.ts:200-211`), so a bare `sangkar status` would record `["budget","paths"]`, the field would be a constant equal to the schema, and it would stop answering the only question it exists for — which arguments a caller actually reaches for. `[]` therefore means "the caller sent no arguments". `budget_tokens` = `Number(spec.args.budget)` when that key is a number, else 0 — the **effective** budget, i.e. after the manifest default; the asymmetry is deliberate, because the budget in force is a fact about the answer while the supplied names are a fact about the request. `status` = `spec.status` (`RecordStatus`): `envelope.status` when there is an envelope, `"absent"` only for a successful answer with no envelope (the CLI's no-graph `status` line), `"error"` when the process refused; a record's status and the process exit code never contradict each other.

`stale`, `stale_reason` come from the envelope with the no-envelope fallback `true`/`null`, but `stale_reason` is recorded as a **closed enum**, never verbatim: `null` when the envelope is absent or its `read_status.stale_reason` is `undefined`; `"other"` when the envelope's string is not in `STALE_REASONS`; otherwise that value. Reason: the envelope types the field as plain `string` (`src/shared/envelope.ts:51`) and six tools write prose into it — `trace` builds `"${node.path}" has no indexed file row` / `… was last written by generation N` (`src/tools/trace/handler.ts:682-683`), `status` composes verdict words (`src/tools/status/handler.ts:252,298,303`), `search` writes a sentence with counts (`src/tools/search/handler.ts:221`), `expand` writes a fixed sentence (`src/tools/expand/handler.ts:539`), `index` writes two (`src/tools/index/handler.ts:282,291`) and `summary` writes two (`src/tools/summary/handler.ts:658,685`) — so a verbatim copy would put a repo path in every stale `call` record. The envelope itself is untouched; this is a recording rule.

`error_code` = `spec.errorCode` (a closed-union `AppError.code`), never a message; `payload_tokens` = envelope === null ? 0 : `Math.ceil(Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify(spec.envelope), "utf8") / BYTES_PER_TOKEN)`; `budget_used_ratio` = budget_tokens === 0 ? null : rounded to 4 decimals; `truncated`/`truncation_reason`/`total_relation`/`coverage_*`/`unresolved_edges` come from the envelope with the no-envelope fallbacks false/null/"gte"/false/0/0/0/0; `generation_age_ms` = `Date.parse(ctx.ts) - Date.parse(read_status.indexed_at)` or null when either is unparseable; `result_counts` = for each top-level key of `envelope.payload` whose value is an array, `key -> array.length` (no payload value is ever copied). For `index_run`: `generation_advanced` = `spec.exitCode === 0`, `unresolved_ratio` = denominator 0 ? 0 : 4-dp ratio of unresolvedCount to unresolvedCount+edges. `repoSizeBucketOf`: bytes <= 0 -> "none", < 1 MiB -> "xs", < 10 MiB -> "s", < 100 MiB -> "m", < 1 GiB -> "l", else "xl". `tool` is copied from `spec.tool`, which the caller has already clamped to a registered name or `"unknown"`. Every field is always present; objects are built as literals in the field order above so a JSON line is diffable.

Do not touch any file other than `src/shared/telemetry/record.ts`.

Create `src/shared/telemetry/identity.ts`. It imports only `node:crypto`, `node:fs`, `node:path`.

export interface InstallIdentity { readonly installId: string; readonly isFirstRun: boolean }
export function resolveInstallId(dir: string, now: Date): InstallIdentity;
export function newSessionId(): string;
export function utcDateOf(date: Date): string;   // "YYYY-MM-DD", the file-name form

`<dir>/install_id.json` holds `{"install_id": string, "date": "YYYY-MM-DD"}`. `resolveInstallId`: read the file; when it parses and its `date` equals `utcDateOf(now)`, return that id with `isFirstRun: false`; otherwise mint `randomBytes(16).toString("hex")`, write the file with flag `"wx"` and `{recursive: true}` on the directory, and return `isFirstRun: true`. An `EEXIST` on the create means another process won the race: read the file back and return whatever it holds with `isFirstRun: false`. Any other read or parse failure is treated as "no usable file" and rotates. `newSessionId` is `randomBytes(16).toString("hex")`.

The module must never throw for a filesystem problem: a write failure is swallowed (the caller's sink is already guarded) and the freshly minted id is returned. It reads no environment variable, no repo path, no hostname and no user identity.

Create `src/shared/telemetry/sink.ts` and `src/shared/telemetry/telemetry.ts`.

`sink.ts` is the seam and owns no policy (Design 1/3). It exports exactly:

export interface TelemetrySink { write(date: string, lines: readonly string[]): void }
export function jsonlSink(dir: string): TelemetrySink;
export function nullSink(): TelemetrySink;

`jsonlSink(dir)` appends `lines.join("\n") + "\n"` to `<dir>/<date>.jsonl` with one `appendFileSync` call, creating `dir` with `mkdirSync(recursive)` on first use. `date` is a `YYYY-MM-DD` string the recorder derives from the record's own `ts`, so the sink does no date arithmetic and holds no clock. `nullSink` discards. Both are pure with respect to the record: the sink never parses, filters or re-serialises a line.

`telemetry.ts` is the recorder. It imports `#shared/config` (type only), `#shared/log` (type only), `./identity`, `./record`, `./sink`, and `node:fs`/`node:path` for the one `statSync`.

export const FLUSH_AT_RECORDS = 32;
export const FLUSH_AT_MS = 5_000;
export interface TelemetryOptions { readonly dir: string; readonly repoRoot: string; readonly dataDir: string; readonly sessionMode: SessionMode; readonly serverVersion: string; readonly graphSchemaVersion: number; readonly sink?: TelemetrySink; readonly now?: () => Date; readonly log?: Logger }
export interface Telemetry { readonly installId: string; readonly sessionId: string; recordCall(spec: CallSpec): void; recordIndexRun(spec: IndexRunSpec): void; flush(): void; close(): void }
export function createTelemetry(options: TelemetryOptions): Telemetry;
export function nullTelemetry(): Telemetry;

Behaviour: `createTelemetry` resolves the install id with `resolveInstallId(options.dir, now())`, mints a session id, builds `RecordContext` (`bunVersion: Bun.version`, `os: process.platform`, `arch: process.arch`, injected `serverVersion`/`graphSchemaVersion`), emits exactly one `session` record through `sessionRecordOf` (`repoSizeBucket: repoSizeBucketOf(statSync(join(dataDir, "graph.db")).size)` with a `0` fallback when the stat throws, `rootKind: rootKindOf(existsSync(join(repoRoot, ".git")))`, `languages: []`), then returns the recorder. `recordCall`/`recordIndexRun` increment `seq` (starting at 1), append the built record to the buffer as its serialised line plus the UTC date of its own `ts` (`utcDateOf` from `./identity`, applied to `new Date(record.ts)`), and call `maybeFlush()`.

`maybeFlush` flushes when the buffer holds >= `FLUSH_AT_RECORDS`, or when `now() - oldestBufferedAt >= FLUSH_AT_MS`; there is no interval timer, so nothing keeps the process alive. `flush` groups the buffer's entries by their date, preserving order, and calls `sink.write(date, lines)` **once per date group** — one append per flush in the normal case, two only for a flush that straddles UTC midnight. That is the concurrency contract: the server (`src/index.ts`) and the worker it spawns (`src/tools/index/handler.ts:155` passes the whole parent environment) hold separate recorders writing the same daily file, so a single `O_APPEND` write of at most 32 joined lines makes interleaving line-atomic and never mid-line. `close` flushes and marks the recorder closed (further records are dropped after one `debug` line).

Every sink call is inside a try/catch: on the first failure log exactly one `warn` (`log.warn("telemetry.sink_failed", { reason })`) and set a `degraded` flag so no further write is attempted and no further warn is emitted for this process. A record is never dropped because of a bad line: the sink serialises what the builder returned, and `JSON.stringify` of a record cannot throw (no cycles, no `BigInt`).

`nullTelemetry()` returns the same interface with empty ids and no-op methods, and is what a caller uses when `config.telemetry.enabled` is false or when no recorder was supplied.

`FLUSH_AT_MS` must be injectable through `now` only — never `Date.now()` directly inside the module — so a test can prove the age trigger without sleeping.

A torn final line (a process killed between `write` and the filesystem) is not repaired here: `report.ts`'s reader counts it `unparsed` and skips it (TASK-012), which is also what makes a lost tail a bounded, documented loss rather than a corrupted file.
